This system is designed to help the Digital team obtain the correct quantity value needed for Pricing-as-a-Service calls. The quantity depends on how a drug has been dispensed in real claims over the last 180 days. EDW already calculates these claim patterns and sends a daily file to our GCS bucket. This file is not raw claim data; instead, it is an aggregated view of claim behavior. Each row in the file represents one unique combination of NDC, GPI, drug name, quantity dispensed, days supply, and delivery channel (retail or mail). For each of those combinations, EDW also includes the total count of paid claims seen in the past 180 days, as well as supporting metadata like the lookback window dates and a change timestamp. For a given NDC, there will be multiple rows in this file because different quantities, day supplies, and channels produce different dispensing patterns. However, for any one exact combination of those six key fields, there is only one row, meaning those six fields serve as a composite primary key.

Every day, EDW uploads a fresh full snapshot file to the designated Google Cloud Storage bucket. The system must immediately detect when a new file arrives. To achieve this, the bucket is configured with a notification that triggers whenever a new object is finalized. That notification is delivered to a Pub/Sub topic. A Cloud Function or Cloud Run service is subscribed to that Pub/Sub topic. When the Pub/Sub message arrives, it contains the bucket name and filename of the newly uploaded EDW file. The Cloud Function or Cloud Run service receives the message, reads the file from the bucket, and begins the ETL process.

The ETL process is simple and strictly follows an upsert strategy. After reading the file, the loader parses each row and checks whether a record with the same six key fields (NDC, GPI, drug name, quantity dispensed, days supply, and delivery channel) already exists in Postgres. If it does exist, the loader updates that row with the latest claim count, timestamp, and any other metadata. If it does not exist, the loader inserts a new row. No records are deleted during this process, even if they disappear from the new file, because the team confirmed that deletion should not be performed. As a result, the Postgres database gradually accumulates all historical combinations, with each row always reflecting the latest claim-count and timestamp based on the most recent EDW file.

This Postgres table serves as the data source for the new API that Digital will call. The API accepts a request where Digital provides one or more NDCs. For each NDC, they specify whether the retail channel should be considered, whether the mail channel should be considered, and a list of day-supply values they want to retrieve quantity information for. The API does not calculate anything; it simply performs a lookup into Postgres using the parameters that Digital provides. When Digital requests, for example, the quantity for a 30-day supply under the retail channel for a particular NDC, the API queries all rows in Postgres matching that NDC, that day-supply value, and that channel. If multiple rows exist for that combination, the API selects the row with the highest claim count, because that quantity has been the most common dispensing pattern in the last 180 days. The quantity from that selected row becomes the “quantity_dispensed” returned to Digital. If no row exists for a particular channel and day-supply combination, the API returns a quantity of zero for that combination. The API repeats this logic for each NDC, each requested day-supply value, and each applicable channel.

Digital uses the returned quantity values to calculate the pricing for that drug. The API does not store cache, nor does it need any. The data changes only once per day, and the database is small and fast enough to query directly. The Digital team will implement any caching they need on their side. The API’s job is simply to read the most recent Postgres data and return the correct quantity for each requested (NDC, day-supply, channel) pair.

From end to end, the system works as follows: EDW sends a daily aggregated claim file into the GCS bucket. The bucket triggers a Pub/Sub message to notify that a new file has arrived. A Cloud Function or Cloud Run service processes the file and performs an upsert into Postgres using the combination of NDC, GPI, drug name, quantity dispensed, days supply, and channel as the natural key. The API receives requests from Digital, queries Postgres based on NDC, day-supply, and channel, selects the row with the highest claim count for each pair, and returns the quantity_dispensed from that row. This is the correct quantity to be used in Digital’s Pricing-as-a-Service API calls.
